-- title:
--   Signed distance to the boundary of a cone
-- statement:
--   Let $E$ be a real inner product space, $v \in E$ a unit vector and $\vartheta$ an apex angle. Decompose a point $p \in E$ into its component along the axis and its component across it: the **transverse part** is
--
--   $$\mathrm{across}(v, p) \;=\; p - \langle v, p\rangle\, v,$$
--
--   so that $a = \langle v, p \rangle$ measures displacement along $v$ and $b = \|\mathrm{across}(v,p)\|$ measures displacement perpendicular to $v$. The **cone gap** of $p$ relative to the cone $\tilde V(v,\vartheta)$ is then
--
--   $$\mathrm{coneGap}(v,\vartheta,p) \;=\; \langle v, p\rangle \sin\vartheta \;-\; \|\mathrm{across}(v,p)\|\,\cos\vartheta \;=\; a \sin\vartheta - b\cos\vartheta .$$
--
--   The module comments record the intended reading: this quantity is positive exactly on the cone $\tilde V(v,\vartheta)$, is $1$-Lipschitz in $p$, is positively homogeneous, and equals $\|p\|\sin\big(\vartheta - \angle(v,p)\big)$, the exact distance from $p$ to the boundary of the cone.
--
--   The value of having the gap as an explicit signed quantity rather than as a membership predicate is quantitative: a ball of radius $\rho < \mathrm{coneGap}(v,\vartheta,p)$ about $p$ lies inside the cone, so cone membership survives perturbation by a computable amount. This is the form in which cone membership is used throughout the later geometric arguments, and it makes the constants there explicit.
--
--   **Formalization Note.** The definitions place no hypotheses at all on $v$ and $\vartheta$ — the axis is not required to be a unit vector and the angle is not required to lie in $(0,\pi/2]$ — so the stated interpretations hold only under those side conditions, supplied where the definitions are used.
